\section{Which one to use}{

\code{dirichlet_revision()} is the safest default when the counts are large.  The
correction applied to a pending report of age \code{j} is
\code{rho(j) = p / (p + (1 - p) * (1 - G_C(j)))}, so at high counts a \emph{shape} error
in \code{g_C} biases the nowcast by more than its Monte-Carlo noise: on a COVID
series of ~8000 cases/day a lognormal \code{g_C} fitted to a \code{1 + Poisson(2)} lag
left a ~0.9\% bias and lost nominal coverage, while the Dirichlet recovered
\code{rho} to four decimals.  At low counts the parametric families are fine and
estimate fewer parameters.
}

